[FINAL-TEXT] **Root cause confirmed.** Router6's startup.log shows the DROP rules were added at boot — permanent, not toggled. FORWARD chain on eth2 drops ALL TCP, UDP, and ICMP from the WAN side. No cron or toggle script found. 28 ICMP packets already hit the drop rule.

This explains the symptom precisely: traffic FROM external subnets arriving at router6 via eth2 (WAN link to router3) gets dropped before reaching pc15-pc18. Return traffic for sessions they initiate also gets dropped. All four users on switch10 (172.18.51.x) affected simultaneously because they share router6 as their WAN gateway.

The "intermittent" description likely comes from users noticing local subnet traffic (172.18.51.x ↔ 172.18.50.x via router6 eth0/eth1) still working, while WAN-side traffic always fails — appearing as selective/intermittent from their perspective.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** iptables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically three DROP rules applied to inbound traffic on eth2 (the WAN interface connecting router6 to router3 via the 10.50.1.0/24 segment).

**Mechanism:** The FORWARD chain contains: one narrow ACCEPT for TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1, followed by DROP rules for all TCP, all UDP, and all other packets (ICMP) arriving on eth2. Because eth2 is the sole WAN path between the 172.18.51.x segment and the rest of the network, these rules silently discard all return traffic for sessions initiated by pc15–pc18, and all traffic from external subnets directed toward them. 28 ICMP packets have already been counted against the catch-all DROP rule.

**Localization:** router6, iptables FORWARD chain, three rules matching `-i eth2`: `-p tcp -j DROP`, `-p udp -j DROP`, and the catch-all `-j DROP`. These were injected by router6's startup script (confirmed in `/var/log/startup.log`) and are permanent — no cron job or toggle script exists.

**Root cause:** router6's startup configuration appended blanket DROP rules for all inbound WAN traffic on eth2, blocking all cross-subnet reachability to and from the 172.18.51.0/24 segment (pc15–pc18) via the wider network.

**Fix:** Remove the three DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain: `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, `iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`. Also remove them from the startup script to prevent recurrence on reboot.
=== END ===